      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50232213",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Most coding agent frameworks fail not because the underlying frontier models are deficient, but because the harnesses feed them overwhelming amounts of raw repository noise. Cupel approaches this bottleneck from a systems perspective by building a modular, multi-crate coding agent harness in Rust focused on context refinement and deterministic execution.\n\nRather than bundling monolithic agent logic, the architecture separates responsibilities cleanly. The core module handles provider-neutral completions, request tracing, and backoff routines. The agent layer manages the prompt loop, message state, and execution hooks, while specialized packages handle code retrieval and language server interactions.\n\nBy treating context compaction as an explicit lifecycle hook rather than an afterthought, the harness actively filters out peripheral file contents before calling inference. If you are building internal developer agents or looking for clean Rust patterns to tame LLM token bloat, inspect this architecture."
