    {
      "title": "Mnemonic proxy offloads agent context without losing verbatim history",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/lastreload/mnemonic-proxy",
      "date": "2026-10-25",
      "authors": [
        "Niko1221"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how transparent proxying can preserve full agent execution history on disk while feeding only relevant context to local models. You will learn a practical architectural pattern to run coding agents indefinitely without context loss.",
      "hn_id": "50231157",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50231157",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Expanding the context window on local models rarely fixes agent degradation over long-running sessions. When history expands, models drown in irrelevant tokens, attention dilutes, and latency explodes.\n\nMnemonic Proxy solves this by sitting between the agent and an OpenAI-compatible backend as a transparent proxy. The agent can submit its entire unmodified history, but the proxy intercepts the payload and prunes old material from the active prompt. Crucially, it does not summarize: every raw message, tool invocation, and tool output is saved to disk verbatim, exposing structured retrieval endpoints that let the agent selectively recall segments when needed.\n\nIn benchmarks, this selective retrieval approach within a modest 32K window consistently outperforms feeding raw hundreds of thousands of tokens directly to the model. Keeping the context window clean maintains deterministic tool calling and eliminates prompt bloat.\n\nOffloading memory to disk instead of forcing the attention mechanism to track everything is a far better blueprint for building resilient autonomous systems."
    },
    {
      "title": "Bbtree delivers massive speedups over standard concurrent ordered maps",
      "source": "github",
      "url": "https://github.com/XiangpengHao/bbtree",
      "date": "2026-10-25",
      "authors": [
        "Xiangpeng Hao"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand how modern database research and formal verification techniques can produce a concurrent ordered map that vastly outperforms standard implementations in speed and latency.",
      "hn_id": "50233213",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50233213",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Standard concurrent ordered maps like skiplists or lock-based B-trees often hit severe scalability bottlenecks under high thread contention. Crossbeam-skiplist is widely treated as the default concurrent ordered map in Rust, but cache locality issues and pointer indirection degrade throughput as core counts scale up.\n\nThe bbtree project presents an alternative concurrent B-tree implementation built on modern database indexing research. Benchmarks demonstrate that it outperforms standard library ordered maps by five times on a single thread and runs twelve times faster than Crossbeam-skiplist at 96 threads. It also decreases memory consumption by thirty percent and delivers a fivefold reduction in tail latency.\n\nWhat makes this engine remarkable is its engineering rigor. The author verified the data structure using formal verification, TLA-style model checking, deterministic simulation, and fuzzing to guarantee memory safety and thread safety under heavy concurrent mutation.\n\nFor backend developers designing in-memory caching layers or high-throughput storage engines, bbtree provides a drop-in replacement that eliminates skiplist lock contention without sacrificing data consistency."
    },
    {
